<TextPage
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Gui collects, why, and how to remove it."
  path="/privacy"
  updated="November 28, 2026"
>
  <p>
    Work In Progress LLC (“WIP”) makes Gui. This page tells you what Gui collects about you, why,
    and how to remove it. If you have a question, send an email to <a href="mailto:hello@usegui.com">hello@usegui.com</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Your projects stay on your computer</h2>
  <p>
    Gui runs your app and your agent on your computer. Gui does not send your code, your files, your
    chats, or your screenshots to us. Gui sends us only two things about how it runs: crash
    reports and usage data, below. You can turn off each of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your agent (for example Claude Code or Codex) sends your prompts and code to the company that
    makes it, under that company’s terms. You sign in to the agent with your own account. Gui does
    not see or keep your agent’s credentials.
  </p>

  <h2>Crash reports</h2>
  <p>
    When Gui has an error or a part of Gui stops, Gui sends a crash report to us. It contains the
    error message, where in Gui’s code the error occurred, the Gui version, the version of macOS
    (on Linux, of the Linux kernel), the type of processor, your country, and the date. Gui
    removes your user name from file paths before it sends the report. A crash report does not contain your code, your files, your chats, or the
    pages that you preview. We use crash reports only to repair Gui. To stop them, turn off Send
    crash reports in Gui, under Settings → Account.
  </p>

  <h2>Usage data</h2>
  <p>
    Gui counts some of the things that you do in it, so that we know which parts of Gui people
    use. Gui sends an event when:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Gui opens. The event contains the Gui version, the operating system (macOS or Linux) and its
      version (on Linux, the Linux kernel version), and the type of processor.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your agent finishes a message. The event contains the agent (only “custom” for an agent that
      you added yourself), whether the message finished, failed, or was stopped, and the model
      provider that you selected for the agent, if the agent has one. It also contains whether the
      message included an element or area that you picked in the preview, whether the agent used
      Gui’s own tools, and whether Ask, Auto, or the agent’s own settings decided what the agent
      can do. If the message failed, the event contains the reason from a fixed list: not signed
      in, agent too old, agent stopped, chat too long, out of credit, servers busy, too many
      requests, server problem, no connection, or other.
    </li>
    <li>
      You open a project. The event contains the framework that starts your app, from a fixed list
      (for example Next.js, Vite, or Astro), “other”, “none” if nothing starts, or “unknown” if you
      have not chosen the app yet.
    </li>
    <li>You make a canvas.</li>
    <li>Your app opens in the preview.</li>
    <li>
      Your app does not start. The event contains the reason from a fixed list: the install failed,
      a command is missing, the start command stopped, or other. Gui sends each reason one time
      each time that you open the project.
    </li>
    <li>You connect an agent, when you install it or sign in to it. The event contains the agent.</li>
    <li>
      Gui cannot use an agent that you installed. The event contains the agent, whether it is the
      agent that you are using, and the reason from a fixed list: not signed in, too old, the agent
      stopped, no connection, the check took too long, or error. Gui sends each agent and reason one
      time each time that Gui opens, and one more time if you then start to use that agent.
    </li>
    <li>Gui updates. The event contains the old version and the new version.</li>
    <li>
      Gui finds an update but cannot download it or install it. The event contains which step
      failed and the reason from a fixed list: no connection, the connection stopped, the server
      was too slow, a server error, the file did not match, the signature did not match, the disk
      is full, no permission, a read-only disk, or other.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each event also contains a random ID that Gui makes for your copy of Gui, your country, and the
    date and time. The random ID is not connected to your Gui account or your email address.
  </p>
  <p>
    Usage data never contains your code, your files, the names or paths of your projects and files,
    your chats, your prompts, what your agent writes, the addresses of the pages that you preview,
    the commands that start your app, error messages, your name, your email address, or your IP
    address.
  </p>
  <p>
    When Gui checks for updates, we count the check. We keep the Gui version, the update channel
    (for example alpha), your country, and the date and time. An update check has no ID.
  </p>
  <p>
    Gui sends usage data to usegui.com, which sends it to PostHog, a service that stores it for us.
    To stop usage data, turn off Share usage data in Gui, under Settings → Account. Gui then also
    deletes the events that it has not sent.
  </p>

  <h2>Your Gui account</h2>
  <p>
    To use Gui, you sign in with Google, GitHub, or a code sent to your email. WorkOS runs the
    sign-in page for us. We keep:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your email address and your name, as the sign-in service gives them to us.</li>
    <li>An ID from WorkOS, so that we know it is you next time.</li>
    <li>When you made the account and when you last signed in.</li>
    <li>
      Whether you choose to get emails about Gui. This is off when you make an account. You can turn it on or off in Settings → Account.
    </li>
    <li>
      A record of each computer that is signed in. We keep only a hash of its sign-in token, not the
      token.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>GitHub</h2>
  <p>
    If you connect GitHub, we keep your GitHub user name and the access tokens that let Gui clone
    the repositories that you choose. We encrypt the tokens. Gui
    gets access only to the repositories that you select on GitHub. You can disconnect GitHub in
    Gui, under Settings → GitHub, and revoke Gui’s access on GitHub.
  </p>

  <h2>Email</h2>
  <p>
    We send sign-in emails when you ask for them. Product emails are optional and off for new accounts. If you choose to receive them in Settings → Account, Work In Progress LLC can send you emails about Gui features, news, and offers. You can turn them off in the same setting or use the unsubscribe link in an email. Sign-in and account emails still come when you need them.
  </p>

  <h2>The website</h2>
  <p>
    If you sign up for the Windows waitlist on usegui.com, we keep your email address, the
    platform, your country, and the date. We use your address only to tell you when Gui is
    available for that platform.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you download Gui from usegui.com, we count the download. We keep the date, your country,
    and the website that sent you (only its name, not the full address). We do not keep your IP
    address, and we cannot use this record to identify you.
  </p>

  <h2>Who processes your data</h2>
  <p>
    The Gui account service and usegui.com run on Cloudflare, which stores the data above, except
    usage data. PostHog stores usage data. WorkOS runs sign-in:
    it keeps a record of your sign-in (your email, your name, and how you sign in) and sends
    sign-in codes. Google and GitHub process your sign-in under their own privacy policies. We do
    not sell your data, and we do not share it for advertising.
  </p>

  <h2>Remove your data</h2>
  <p>
    To delete your Gui account and all the data above, send an email to{' '}
    <a href="mailto:hello@usegui.com">hello@usegui.com</a> from the address on your account. Signing
    out in Gui removes the sign-in from that computer only. To remove your address from the waitlist,
    send an email to the same address.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>If we change this page, we change the date at the top.</p>
</TextPage>
